Ticket **#4471** was marked known as one parser bug: the shared parser reads dates
month first, and day-first tenants get a flipped day. The export of the 412 merged
reports does not support that.

`causeOf` in `#lib/kb/causes` places each report from the **tenant** (locale + booking
form) and the **channel**, not from `meta.intakeTag`. Against that rule, #4471 holds
**two** causes.

## What the merge actually holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The two problems

1. **parser** (379 reports) — day-first tenants on app, email, or booking-v2 form:
   the shared parser still reads month first, so the day and month swap.
2. **hint** (33 reports) — day-first tenants on **booking-v3** through the **form**
   channel only (`alder.physio`, `copper.studio`, `pale.harbour`): the form prints
   `MM/DD/YYYY` under the field, people type month first, and the form reads the
   tenant's day-first order. That is a different bug from the shared parser.

Intake tagged many of these the other way (`parser` vs `hint`). The cause follows
the tenant, so those tags are guesses — not the ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one known issue. Split it: one ticket per cause above, with the report
counts from the table. Do not merge on subject alone when the causes differ.
</Callout>

## Why the old page was wrong

The incident page said "one issue, one fix queued" and status **known**. The table on
that same page already computed two causes; the prose did not match what the data
showed. A number the code can compute belongs in a component — and then the words
have to agree with it.

Five reports in `desk-notes.json` (`oneDayMove`) were read as a one-day shift. By
tenant they still land under **parser**; they are a footnote, not a third cause.

